        },
        {
          "text": "1 Ideal, theoretical average",
          "bbox": [
            0.4925,
            0.712406,
            0.5075,
            0.87782
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 785,
          "end": 813
        },
        {
          "text": "If information regarding the filtrate flow as a function of membrane position cannot be",
          "bbox": [
            0.539,
            0.140977,
            0.55814,
            0.834586
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 814,
          "end": 901
        },
        {
          "text": "obtained or approximated, the VCF data presented in Table 2.1 may be used as an estimate for",
          "bbox": [
            0.558,
            0.139098,
            0.574128,
            0.883459
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 902,
          "end": 994
        },
        {
          "text": "NF/RO systems. However, it is strongly recommended that Equation 2.24, 2.26, or 2.27 be used",
          "bbox": [
            0.573005,
            0.123969,
            0.593875,
            0.881662
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 995,
          "end": 1087
        },
        {
          "text": "to obtain a more accurate, system-specific estimate for the average VCF. Alternatively, the",
          "bbox": [
            0.5915,
            0.161654,
            0.609012,
            0.885338
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 1088,
          "end": 1179
        },
        {
          "text": "maximum VCF （i.e. the most conservative value） can be calculated from the overall recovery",
          "bbox": [
            0.6075,
            0.142857,
            0.628,
            0.883459
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 1180,
          "end": 1270
        },
        {
          "text": "uSing Equation 2.25.",
          "bbox": [
            0.62936,
            0.718045,
            0.647,
            0.883459
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 1271,
          "end": 1291
        },
        {
          "text": "2.5.2.2 Crossflow Model",
          "bbox": [
            0.6815,
            0.590226,
            0.696221,
            0.825188
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 1292,
          "end": 1315
        },
        {
          "text": "The objective of crossflow filtration is to maintain a high scour velocity across the",
          "bbox": [
            0.7165,
            0.176692,
            0.731105,
            0.823308
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 1316,
          "end": 1401
        },
        {
          "text": "membrane surface to minimize particle deposition and membrane fouling. Crossflow membrane",
          "bbox": [
            0.734,
            0.12406,
            0.748547,
            0.881579
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 1402,
          "end": 1491
        },
        {
          "text": "processes operate in an unsteady-state manner in which suspended solids accumulate on the feed",
          "bbox": [
            0.751453,
            0.12406,
            0.766,
            0.881579
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 1492,
          "end": 1586
        },
        {
          "text": "side of the membrane over the course of a filtration cycle. Thus, in crossflow systems, the VCF",
          "bbox": [
            0.767442,
            0.12594,
            0.7835,
            0.883459
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 1587,
          "end": 1682
        },
        {
          "text": "varies as a function of time. At the end of each filtration cycle the membrane unit is backwashed",
          "bbox": [
            0.786337,
            0.120301,
            0.801,
            0.881579
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 1683,
          "end": 1780
        },
        {
          "text": "to remove the accumulated solids. Crossflow filtration has traditionally been used in conjunction",
          "bbox": [
            0.803779,
            0.118421,
            0.821221,
            0.885338
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 1781,
          "end": 1878
        },
        {
          "text": "with inside-out hollow-fiber membrane processes to increase the scouring velocity in the fiber",
          "bbox": [
            0.821,
            0.142857,
            0.836,
            0.881579
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 1879,
          "end": 1973
        },
        {
          "text": "lumen in order to minimize fouling.",
          "bbox": [
            0.835757,
            0.599384,
            0.853366,
            0.883647
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 1974,
          "end": 2009
        },
        {
          "text": "For crossflow systems, a portion of the concentrate stream （i.e.， the tangential flow） is",
          "bbox": [
            0.8735,
            0.148496,
            0.888081,
            0.823308
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
          "start": 2010,
          "end": 2099
        },
        {
          "text": "recirculated or recycled to the system inlet and mixed with the incoming feed stream. Because",
          "bbox": [
            0.890988,
            0.137218,
            0.905523,
            0.883459
          ],
          "confidence": 0.5,
          "kind": "text",
